cautions.

\paragraph{Stage C --- Meta-rule reflection.} From each domain we
balance-sample up to $K{=}10$ cases ($K/2$ A-wins, $K/2$ B-wins;
deterministic, sorted by item id) and feed them in a single
multi-image prompt to a reflector VLM (Qwen3.5-VL-27B). The reflector
sees per case the query image, both branches' score--rationale pairs,
and the ground-truth label. (Reference images are omitted because
vLLM caps image count at $12$ per prompt; refs are implicitly
described by each branch's rationale.) The reflector is asked for
$1$--$3$ routing rules per side under three explicit constraints:
(i) each rule must name an \emph{observable} pattern in the
rationale or image, not merely cite the item id; (ii) no ``always
trust $X$'' rules; (iii) rules must be actionable at inference time
from what the Controller sees. All $12$ domains produced parseable
rule lists, yielding $2$--$3$ rules per side per domain.

\paragraph{Stage D --- Rule stacking.} For each domain we merge the
meta-rules with the domain rules from the reference-only + oracle
pilot: reference-only invariants (\texttt{invariant} tag, six types:
count, symmetry, spatial layout, colour palette, texture,
structural) and oracle-grounded cluster rules
(\texttt{corrective\_fn} / \texttt{corrective\_fp} tags). Each rule
carries $(\mathrm{type}, \mathrm{category}, \mathrm{text},
\mathrm{source\_items}, \mathrm{confidence})$ metadata. Per-domain
stacked rulebook sizes range from $10$ (D12 road safety) to $86$
(D1 MVTec-AD, many categories each contributing invariants).

\subsection{Results}
\label{ssec:ctrl_results}

\paragraph{Setup.} All numbers are on CrossDomainVAD-12 manifests\_v2
test ($n{=}1418$), Qwen3.5-VL-27B served locally (4 DP replicas
behind a round-robin load balancer), $\texttt{DESCRIPTOR\_MODE}=$\texttt{generic}
throughout, $\max\_\text{turns}=3$ for the v9 trajectory.
\emph{Paired} stratified bootstrap CIs ($1000$ resamples, per-domain
stratification) compare v11 against the v10 blend recomputed from the
same run's $(s_A, s_B)$ --- an exact paired comparison on identical
branch outputs.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{\textbf{v11 Controller Learning on Qwen3.5-VL-27B
CrossDomainVAD-12 test} ($n{=}1418$). Baseline is v10
($w_A = w_B = 0.5$) which matches Table~\ref{tab:main_results}'s
reported $0.732$ within $0.13$ pp ($0.7333$ on this run). Paired
bootstrap CI on the macro $\Delta$ is $[+0.41, +3.67]$ pp,
$\hat p(\Delta{>}0) = 0.995$ --- significant at $95\%$.}
\label{tab:controller_main}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Regime & Macro AUROC & $\Delta$ vs v10 & $95\%$ CI & $\hat p(\Delta{>}0)$\\
\midrule
Single-call Direct (generic) & $0.7119$ & $-2.14$ & --- & --- \\
Agent v9 alone               & $0.6696$ & $-6.37$ & --- & --- \\
v10 blend = Passive v11      & $0.7333$ & ---     & --- & --- \\
v11 Controller (meta only)   & $0.7396$ & $+0.63$ & $[-1.26, +2.36]$ & $0.730$ \\
v11 Controller (meta+domain) & $\mathbf{0.7539}$ & $\mathbf{+2.05}$ & $[+0.41, +3.67]$ & $\mathbf{0.995}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\\[4pt]
{\footnotesize Ablation comparison: the full stack (meta $\cup$ domain
rules) contributes $+1.43$ pp incremental over meta-rules alone
(bootstrap mean; CI $[-0.19, +3.13]$, $\hat p{=}0.960$). Meta-rules
alone are directionally positive but the CI straddles zero --- the
gain is carried jointly by routing rules \emph{and} domain content.}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Per-domain breakdown.}
Table~\ref{tab:controller_per_domain} reports per-domain
v10-blend AUROC, v11 AUROC, and the Controller's categorical
\texttt{trust} output counts.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\footnotesize
\caption{\textbf{Per-domain Controller behaviour.} Columns: test $n$,
v10 blend AUROC (recomputed from the same branch outputs), v11
Controller AUROC, $\Delta$ in percentage points, then the Controller's
categorical \texttt{trust} decision counts (Agent / Direct / Blend).
Wins on $8$ of $12$ domains; three losses (D3, D9, D12) each $\le\,3.3$ pp;
three big wins (D7, D8 in particular) more than cover the losses.}
\label{tab:controller_per_domain}
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrrrr}
\toprule
Dom & Source & $n$ & v10 & v11 & $\Delta$ (pp) & Trust$_A$ & Trust$_B$ & Blend\\
\midrule
D1 & MVTec-AD          & 120 & 0.934 & 0.938 & $+0.5$        &  9 & 47 & 64 \\
D2 & GoodsAD           & 120 & 0.607 & 0.613 & $+0.6$        & 56 & 17 & 47 \\
D3 & VisA              & 120 & 0.853 & 0.834 & $-1.9$        &  9 & 60 & 51 \\
D4 & SDNET             & 120 & 0.670 & 0.704 & $+3.4$        & 24 & 48 & 48 \\
D5 & MVTec-LOCO        & 120 & 0.676 & 0.678 & $+0.2$        & 28 & 64 & 28 \\
D6 & Real3D-AD         & 120 & 0.531 & 0.558 & $+2.8$        & 33 & 12 & 75 \\
D7 & LEVIR-CD+         &  98 & 0.754 & 0.888 & $\mathbf{+13.4}$ & 24 & 55 & 19 \\
D8 & DermaMNIST        & 120 & 0.647 & 0.721 & $\mathbf{+7.4}$  & 48 & 51 & 21 \\
D9 & BraTS             & 120 & 0.848 & 0.816 & $-3.2$        & 34 & 35 & 51 \\
D10 & BMAD-Liver CT    & 120 & 0.491 & 0.526 & $+3.5$        & 53 & 24 & 43 \\
D11 & HyperKvasir      & 120 & 0.808 & 0.807 & $-0.1$        & 27 & 61 & 32 \\
D12 & BDD+RoadAnomaly  & 120 & 0.982 & 0.963 & $-1.9$        & 11 & 47 & 62 \\
\midrule
\textbf{Macro} & & 1418 & \textbf{0.7333} & \textbf{0.7539} & $\mathbf{+2.05}$ & \multicolumn{3}{c}{---} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Findings.}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Controller arbitration beats fixed blend at $95\%$
significance.} The $+2.05$ pp macro gain has a paired bootstrap
CI of $[+0.41, +3.67]$ and posterior mass $\hat p(\Delta{>}0) =
0.995$. Gains are concentrated but not fabricated: of the $12$
domains, $8$ improve (four by at least $+2.5$ pp), three regress by
at most $3.3$ pp, and one (D11) is essentially neutral. The gain
magnitude is similar to the fixed-blend gain itself ($+1.69$ pp,
Table~\ref{tab:main_results}), yet cumulative --- v11 compounds on top
of v10 rather than replacing it.

\item \textbf{The $+13.4$ pp D7 LEVIR change-detection swing comes
from correct routing.} On remote-sensing change detection, the v9
trajectory scores $0.606$ macro AUROC (systematic FP on seasonal
vegetation changes) while Direct scores $0.861$. The fixed blend
($0.754$) sits roughly halfway, partly collapsing Direct's signal.
The Controller (trust counts $24$ A / $55$ B / $19$ blend) correctly
leans on Direct, and lands at $0.888$ --- a higher AUROC than either
branch alone. D8 DermaMNIST is the same story on dermatology
(v11 $0.721$ vs blend $0.647$, $+7.4$ pp).

\item \textbf{Three regressions, each a known failure mode.}
D3 VisA ($-1.9$ pp): the Controller over-trusts Direct ($60$ B /
$120$ items) when the Agent's structural reasoning was already the
right signal. D9 BraTS ($-3.2$ pp): brain-MRI texture heuristics in
the rulebook push the Controller off-target; \textit{agree-wrong}
items dominate error here so the Controller has no routing signal
to learn from. D12 BDD+RA ($-1.9$ pp): the blend sits at $0.982$,
essentially at the ceiling --- any Controller-driven deviation has
negative expectation. These three cases bound the
honesty of our claim: the $+2.05$ pp macro gain is not uniform.

\item \textbf{Controller behaviour is domain-aware and
non-trivial.} Trust counts vary dramatically: D1 mostly keeps blend
($64/120$), D5 heavily trusts Direct ($64$), D10 heavily trusts
Agent ($53$). The Controller is not collapsing to a constant
preference. Parse-failure rate is exactly $0$ across $1418$ items.

\item \textbf{Meta-rules alone are insufficient; domain content
matters.} Stripping the domain layer (invariants $+$ FN/FP
correctives) and keeping only the routing meta-rules produces a
macro $\Delta$ of only $+0.63$ pp (CI $[-1.26, +2.36]$,
$\hat p{=}0.730$) --- directionally positive but not significant.
The incremental contribution of domain rules is $+1.43$ pp on
average (CI $[-0.19, +3.13]$, $\hat p{=}0.960$). Per-domain, this
splits cleanly: on domains where v9 and Direct disagree in
stereotyped ways (D2 retail, D7 LEVIR, D8 DermaMNIST, D10 liver CT),
full $>$ meta because the Controller needs domain knowledge to
adjudicate; on domains where the two branches are already close
(D1 MVTec, D3 VisA, D5 LOCO, D11 HyperKvasir), meta-only suffices
or is even slightly better (noise level). The worst case for

