paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:29:\caption{\textbf{Main results: per-domain AUROC across three VLM backbones on CrossDomainVAD-12 test} ($n{=}1{,}418$). For each backbone we report the descriptor-free Direct VLM baseline and our \emph{agent+Direct ensemble} ($s = 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{agent}}$, weights frozen a priori), where the agent is \textbf{v10} = v9 refutation agent (\S\ref{ssec:refutation}) wrapped with a parallel independent Direct call; both scores are computed in a single \texttt{agent\_v10.py} invocation per item. Both Direct and agent use \texttt{DESCRIPTOR\_MODE=generic} prompts (no domain hint), matching v9's descriptor-free task preamble for apples-to-apples comparison. \textbf{Macro} row at the bottom; $\Delta$ is ensemble macro minus Direct macro. 95\% stratified paired bootstrap CIs on macro $\Delta$ are reported under the table; the gain is significant at $95\%$ on GPT-5.4 and SeedVL ($P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}1.000$), positive but just above the significance boundary on Qwen3.5 ($P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}0.971$, lower CI $-0.08$ pp touches zero).}
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:59:The \emph{agent-alone vs Direct} comparison tells a richer story: on GPT-5.4 and SeedVL the v9 agent by itself already beats Direct ($+3.64$~pp and $+3.68$~pp respectively, both $P{\ge}0.998$), and the Direct branch of v10 adds a further $+0.4$--$+2.3$~pp on top. On Qwen3.5-VL-27B the v9 agent alone is \emph{significantly weaker} than Direct ($-4.46$~pp, $95\%$ CI $[-7.61, -1.55]$, $P{=}0.001$), yet composing it with an independent Direct call still produces a positive ensemble macro gain. This failure-mode robustness is the motivation for v10's always-on parallel Direct branch rather than a pure agent system. Per-backbone per-domain agent-alone and Direct-alone scores are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}.}
