Score: 7.2/10
Verdict: almost
Verified claims: W2 is substantively fixed: `controller_learning.tex` now consistently states the oracle budget as one 40-item dev pool per domain, with L2 K=10 drawn from that same pool rather than additional labels. W3 is substantively fixed: the paper now separates Controller parse failures `0/1418`, Direct `<=1%`, and v9 trajectory failures around `7%`, and I reproduced the clean-subset sensitivity: n=1318, Δ +1.73 pp, CI [+0.07,+3.29], P=0.982. W4 is fixed: novelty is now framed as modest controller/meta-routing plus reused domain-content rules, not a wholly new learned system. W6 is fixed in the §4 text: “posterior mass” has been replaced by bootstrap sign probability. W5 is partially fixed: the no-rules ablation exists, the raw D4 file is valid, and I reproduced the four-way table exactly: no-rules macro 0.7275, D4 no-rules Δ -11.75 pp, full Δ +2.05 pp, full vs no-rules +2.67 pp CI [+0.78,+4.36].

Unverified / false claims: The current title remains false for the full manuscript if v11 is part of the contribution: “Training-Free” conflicts with §4’s explicitly semi-supervised controller. The abstract, introduction, and conclusion are still v10-only and do not integrate v11; they also still say the v10 ensemble “significantly beats Direct on every backbone” even though Qwen3.5 has CI [-0.08,+3.51], so that is not 95% significant. The ablation decomposition is not as clean as the prose claims: meta-only/no-rules/full were rerun with different upstream Direct/v9 outputs, not replayed on frozen branch outputs. I found branch-score differences on 655/1418 meta-only items and 646/1418 no-rules items relative to full. Thus “exact paired comparison on identical branch outputs” is true for full-vs-blend, but false for the cross-regime ablation interpretation. Relatedly, meta-only “not significant vs v10” depends on comparing it to the full run’s blend baseline; against its own rerun blend, meta-only is +1.86 pp with CI [+0.17,+3.55], P=0.985. This does not invalidate the full result, but it weakens the claimed mechanism decomposition.

Weaknesses (ranked, with minimum fix each)
1. Whole-paper framing is still inconsistent. Minimum fix: retitle or subtitle the paper so v10 is training-free and v11 is a semi-supervised extension, then update abstract/intro/conclusion to include v11 or explicitly mark §4 as an optional extension. Remove “significantly beats Direct on every backbone”; say Qwen is mean-positive but its 95% CI touches zero.
2. The rule ablations are not branch-frozen. Minimum fix: cache one set of v9/direct branch outputs and replay only the Controller under no-rules, meta-only, and meta+domain prompts. Recompute all four-way paired bootstraps on identical branch inputs. Without this, W5 is directionally answered but not cleanly isolated.
3. The +2.05 pp result is defensible at 95%, but fragile. Minimum fix: keep the clean-subset result in the main text or table footnote, and change the stale limitation sentence that still says an “oracle ablation excluding v9 errors” is future work. The remaining clean gain is real but barely clears zero.
4. The stronger mechanistic claim should be narrowed. Minimum fix: say “the full rulebook improves over a no-rules controller; current ablations suggest rules carry the gain, pending frozen-branch replay.” Do not claim meta-rules alone are insufficient until the branch-frozen replay resolves the own-baseline inconsistency.
5. v11 is currently single-backbone evidence. Minimum fix: either label it as a Qwen3.5 case study/extension, or run v11 full and no-rules on at least one additional backbone. A top-venue reviewer will otherwise ask why the new headline section appears only on the backbone where v10 is weakest.
6. Reused v3 domain rules remain a contamination/narrative risk. Minimum fix: make the source, construction budget, and immutability of the reused rulebooks auditable in the appendix, and add at least one negative control such as wrong-domain, shuffled-rule, or length-matched no-op rules if compute permits.

Memory update for Round 7 (if any): Round 6 score 7.2, verdict almost. Core v11 full result is reproducible and defensible at 95%; W2/W3/W4/W6 are fixed. W5 is only partially fixed because no-rules/meta/full ablations were not run on frozen branch outputs. Round 7 should prioritize: frozen-branch controller replay, title/abstract/intro/conclusion rewrite, removal of “significant on every backbone,” and clearer v11-as-semi-supervised-extension framing.
